     "page": 1,
     "text": "Robinson-Holland Clinic\nRe: Jonathan Malone (DOB 12/05/1992)\n\nDear Insurer,\n\nI am writing to provide a comprehensive medical overview of Jonathan Malone, who has been under my care since 2022. Jonathan was diagnosed with Attention Deficit Hyperactivity Disorder (ADHD) in May 2022, and since then, he has been managed effectively with guanfacine 2 mg daily. \n\nDecember 21, 2025: Jonathan was seen today for follow-up regarding his ADHD. His blood pressure was 130/86, and his weight was 198 pounds. His ADHD is currently well-controlled with his current medication regimen. Jonathan reports no new symptoms and has been compliant with his treatment plan. He denies any recent episodes of hyperactivity, impulsivity, or inattentiveness.\n\nOctober 02, 2024: Jonathan visited due to a back strain. At that time, his blood pressure was 128/81, and his weight was 188 pounds. He continues to manage his ADHD effectively, with no reported complications. He mentioned that the back strain was due to lifting heavy objects at work, but it resolved without any further issues. His ADHD remains stable, with no changes in his medication.\n\nJuly 15, 2023: Jonathan's visit was for a routine check-up regarding his ADHD. His blood pressure was 122/79, and he weighed 204 pounds. Jonathan was doing well, with no reported side effects from his medication. He continues to experience significant improvement in his ADHD symptoms. He maintains his focus and organizational skills with the help of his medication. His vital signs were stable, and he had no complaints other than the ongoing management of his ADHD.\n\nJonathan's family history includes his brother, who has Coronary Artery Disease (CAD), and his mother, who had Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD) but unfortunately passed away. Jonathan's brother is currently 62 years old and is doing well, while his mother was diagnosed with COPD 64 and passed away at an unknown age.\n\nSocial history reveals that Jonathan is a former cigarette smoker who smoked 10 cigarettes per day, starting in November 2009. He quit smoking on December 26, 2010. Additionally, Jonathan consumes approximately four alcoholic drinks per week.\n\nJonathan is compliant with his medication regimen and has not reported any adverse effects from guanfacine. His ADHD is well-managed, and he does not have any new symptoms or complications.\n\nPlease let me know if you require any further information or additional documentation.\n\nSincerely,\n\nDr. Timothy Carter\nPsychiatrist\nRobinson-Holland Clinic\n"
    }
